@page "/shell"

@*
    No render mode, and that is the demonstration. Every component on this page - and the shell
    wrapping it - is static HTML and CSS. The drawer this page describes is the one framing it:
    narrow the window and use the menu button in the bar above.
*@

<PageTitle>Shell &middot; ZenithUI</PageTitle>

<header class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Chrome and layout</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-content-muted">
        The five components that frame an application. They are already framing this page:
        <code>MainLayout.razor</code> is a <code>ZenAppShell</code>, and it carries
        <strong>no render mode at all</strong>. A layout cannot have one &mdash;
        <code>@@Body</code> is a <code>RenderFragment</code>, and a render fragment cannot cross a
        render-mode boundary &mdash; so a shell that needed interactivity to open its own
        navigation could not be used as a layout, which is the only place a shell goes.
    </p>
</header>

<!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------- The drawer -->
<section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">The drawer is a popover</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-content-muted">
        <code>ZenSideNav</code> renders one <code>&lt;aside&gt;</code>, not a rail and a drawer.
        Above <code>lg</code> it is a sticky column; below it, <code>popover="auto"</code> takes
        over and the app bar's menu button is a bare <code>&lt;button popovertarget&gt;</code>.
        <strong>Narrow this window below 1024px</strong> and open it: the top layer, the scrim,
        Escape, click-outside dismissal and focus returning to the button are all the browser's,
        with no JavaScript loaded and no circuit open.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <ZenCard Title="What the platform gives" TitleLevel="3">
            <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-content-muted">
                <li>Toggling from any button in the document, by id</li>
                <li>The top layer, so no ancestor's <code>overflow</code> can clip it</li>
                <li><code>::backdrop</code>, with no extra element</li>
                <li>Escape, and dismissal on a click outside</li>
                <li>Focus returned to the button that opened it</li>
                <li>
                    A closed drawer removed from the tab order, because the UA hides an unopened
                    popover with <code>display: none</code>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </ZenCard>

        <ZenCard Title="What a render mode adds" TitleLevel="3">
            <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-content-muted">
                <li>A focus trap &mdash; <code>popover="auto"</code> does not confine Tab</li>
                <li>A scroll lock on the page behind</li>
                <li>
                    Dismissal when a link inside is followed under enhanced navigation, which
                    swaps the page without any event the platform treats as a dismissal
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-content-subtle">
                All three are installed by one call to <code>bindDrawer</code> and are absent, not
                broken, without it.
            </p>
        </ZenCard>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------- Nav links -->
<section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">ZenNavMenu and ZenNavLink</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-content-muted">
        A menu is a <strong>list</strong>, so a screen reader announces &ldquo;list, 5
        items&rdquo; and offers to skip it. A link sets <code>aria-current="page"</code> when it
        matches the location &mdash; Blazor's own <code>NavLink</code> decides the same thing and
        spends it on a CSS class only, so the active item is visible to sighted users and
        announced to nobody.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <ZenCard Title="Vertical, grouped" TitleLevel="3">
            <ZenNavMenu Title="Workspace" Landmark="false">
                <ZenNavLink Href="/shell" Text="Overview" Icon="@ZenIcons.Inbox" />
                <ZenNavLink Href="/shell/reports" Text="Reports" Icon="@ZenIcons.TrendUp">
                    <Trailing>
                        <ZenBadge Intent="ZenIntent.Primary" Size="ZenSize.Small">4</ZenBadge>
                    </Trailing>
                </ZenNavLink>
                <ZenNavLink Href="/shell/billing" Text="Billing" Icon="@ZenIcons.Calendar" Disabled="true" />
            </ZenNavMenu>

            <ZenNavMenu Title="Account" Landmark="false">
                <ZenNavLink Href="/shell/profile" Text="Profile" Icon="@ZenIcons.Success" />
                <ZenNavLink Href="/shell/settings" Text="Settings" Icon="@ZenIcons.Monitor" />
            </ZenNavMenu>

            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-content-subtle">
                &ldquo;Overview&rdquo; is current: this page is <code>/shell</code>. A
                <code>Title</code> also names its <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> landmark, which is how
                a grouped side nav gets distinguishable regions rather than three announcements
                of &ldquo;navigation&rdquo;.
            </p>
        </ZenCard>

        <ZenCard Title="Horizontal" TitleLevel="3">
            <ZenNavMenu Orientation="ZenNavOrientation.Horizontal" Landmark="false">
                <ZenNavLink Href="/shell" Text="Overview" />
                <ZenNavLink Href="/shell/activity" Text="Activity" />
                <ZenNavLink Href="/shell/members" Text="Members" />
                <ZenNavLink Href="/shell/settings" Text="Settings" />
            </ZenNavMenu>

            <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-content-muted">
                Matching follows <code>NavLink</code>'s rules, with one addition: a link with no
                <code>Match</code> set picks its own. Exact for the application root, prefix for
                everything else &mdash; because a root link defaulting to prefix matches every
                page in the app, which is how a hand-written nav ends up with two items
                highlighted.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-content-subtle">
                The prefix test requires a path separator, so <code>/order</code> does not match
                <code>/orders</code>; and a query string is ignored unless the link carries one,
                so a paged table does not deselect its own nav item on page two.
            </p>
        </ZenCard>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------- App bar -->
<section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">ZenAppBar</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-content-muted">
        Four slots &mdash; <code>Brand</code>, <code>ChildContent</code>, <code>Actions</code> and
        an optional <code>Below</code> row that stays inside the sticky region. The bar at the top
        of this page is one; here is another, with a second row.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-zen-lg border border-border">
        <ZenAppBar Sticky="false" ShowMenuToggle="false" Class="relative">
            <Brand>
                <span class="grid size-7 place-items-center rounded-zen-md bg-accent text-sm text-accent-content">N</span>
                <span>Northwind</span>
            </Brand>

            <ChildContent>
                <ZenNavMenu Orientation="ZenNavOrientation.Horizontal" Label="Example sections" Class="ms-2">
                    <ZenNavLink Href="/shell" Text="Dashboard" />
                    <ZenNavLink Href="/shell/orders" Text="Orders" />
                    <ZenNavLink Href="/shell/catalog" Text="Catalog" />
                </ZenNavMenu>
            </ChildContent>

            <Actions>
                <ZenBadge Intent="ZenIntent.Success" Variant="ZenVariant.Soft">Live</ZenBadge>
                <ZenButton Size="ZenSize.Small" Intent="ZenIntent.Primary">New order</ZenButton>
            </Actions>

            <Below>
                <p class="text-sm text-content-subtle">
                    A <code>Below</code> row: a breadcrumb, a filter strip, a narrow-screen nav.
                </p>
            </Below>
        </ZenAppBar>

        <div class="bg-surface px-4 py-8 text-sm text-content-muted sm:px-6">
            Page content would sit here.
        </div>

        <ZenFooter Filled="true" Bordered="true">
            <Start>
                <p>&copy; 2026 Northwind Traders</p>
            </Start>
            <End>
                <a href="/shell" class="zen-focus rounded-zen-sm hover:text-content">Privacy</a>
                <a href="/shell" class="zen-focus rounded-zen-sm hover:text-content">Terms</a>
            </End>
        </ZenFooter>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------- Skip link -->
<section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">The skip link</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-content-muted">
        <strong>Press Tab now</strong>, from the top of this page. The first stop is a
        &ldquo;Skip to main content&rdquo; link that is invisible until it takes focus. It points
        at the shell's <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, which carries <code>tabindex="-1"</code> &mdash;
        without that the browser scrolls to the landmark and leaves focus where it was, so the
        next Tab goes back to the second nav link, which is precisely what the skip link exists to
        avoid.
    </p>
</section>
